course: dictionary
lesson: 11084
title: Winds named by where they come from
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A wind is named for the direction it comes from, not where it is going. A westerly comes from the west, so it blows towards the east. «In the middle latitudes, such as over Britain, the main winds blow from the west and are called the westerlies.»
explain: Westerly can also tell you a direction. «Sailing in a westerly direction» means heading towards the west. Look at the clues in the sentence: if the wind is westerly, ask which way it is coming from; if a path is westerly, ask which way it is going.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Britain gets westerly winds from the Atlantic» becomes «Britain gets winds from the west, from the Atlantic». That still makes sense, so you have understood the word.
fact: A westerly is a wind that blows from the west.
fact: Westerly can also describe a direction: towards the west or in the west.
fact: The westerlies are strong winds that blow from the west in the middle parts of the Earth.
fact: Britain is much milder in winter than Labrador in Canada, partly because westerly winds from the Atlantic reach Britain.
fact: Between about 40 and 50 degrees south, strong westerly winds are called the Roaring Forties.
exercise: A westerly wind blows from which direction? | From the north | *From the west | From the east | From the south
exercise: «The ship sailed in a westerly direction.» Which way was the ship heading? | Towards the east | Straight up | Round in a circle | *Towards the west
exercise: Dutch ships used strong westerly winds to race east from the Cape of Good Hope. Which way did the wind blow? | *From the west | From the north | From the east | From the south
test: What are the westerlies? | Winds that blow from the east in the tropics | Calm patches at the equator | *Strong winds that blow from the west in the middle parts of the Earth
test: Britain is milder in winter than Labrador partly because westerly winds reach it from which ocean? | Arctic | Pacific | *Atlantic | Indian
exam: Which winds blow over places like Britain? | *The westerlies | The trade winds | Calm patches at the equator | The wild winds of the far north
